synopsis:           Type class-driven prelude with lens, comonads, and safe alternatives
category:           Prelude
description:
  <<https://logo.systemf.com.au/systemf-450x450.png>>
  .
  Papa is a Haskell prelude that replaces the partial functions of Prelude
  with total ones, generalises the familiar names to type classes, and
  brings in lens, semigroupoids, bifunctors and comonad.
  .
  = Total replacements
  .
  Total division takes a @NonZero@ divisor, checked once by
  @preview nonZero@ (@divNonZero@, @divideNonZero@, @ratioNonZero@, ...), and
  @pow@ a @Natural@ exponent, and @intCast@ converts between integral types
  only where no value can be lost; Prelude's partial versions, with
  @fromIntegral@, are in the separate module @Papa.Unsafe.Numeric@. Elsewhere, where Prelude throws, Papa returns a
  @Maybe@: @cycle@ (empty list), @(!!)@, @(!?)@ (index out of range), @chr@,
  @succ@, @pred@ (out of range), @stimes@ (count out of range) and @readIO@
  (no parse). On any @Foldable1@, @maximum@, @minimum@, @maximumBy@ and
  @minimumBy@ need no @Maybe@, and @head@, @tail@, @last@ and @init@ are
  lenses into a @NonEmpty@.
  .
  @fromJust@, @read@, @error@, @undefined@, @throw@, @foldl1@, @foldr1@,
  @truncate@, @round@, @realToFrac@ and every @unsafe*@ function are not
  exported (@truncateMaybe@, @roundMaybe@, @floatToDouble@, ... instead); a
  test fails to compile if they are.
  .
  = Text first, and I/O errors as values
  .
  @putStrLn@, @readFile@, @print@, @lines@, @words@ and the rest are
  @Text@'s, read and write UTF-8 whatever the locale, read strictly, and
  return their @IOException@ in @IOExcept@ (@ExceptT IOException IO@) rather
  than throw it. @catch@, @try@ and @bracket@ never catch an asynchronous
  exception. The functions of text, bytestring, containers, vector and
  unordered-containers are wrapped, imported qualified, with a total
  function of the same name for each one that throws.
  .
  = Generalised names
  .
  @map@ is @fmap@, @(++)@ is @(<>)@, @const@ is @pure@, @mapM@ is
  @traverse@, @fst@ and @snd@ work on any tuple, @lookup@ and
  @partitionEithers@ work on any @Foldable@, and @lefts@ and @rights@ are
  traversals.
  .
  = Usage
  .
  > {-# LANGUAGE NoImplicitPrelude #-}
  > import Papa
  >
  > average :: [Int] -> Maybe Int
  > average xs = fmap (divNonZero (sum xs)) (preview nonZero (length xs))
  .
  See "Papa" for the modules that import alongside it, and those to import
  qualified.
